{"id":"h4-01-c1","title":"Caching fundamentals","scopeHtml":"<p>Scope: cache-aside, read/write-through, write-back, eviction, TTL, invalidation and hit-ratio math. Questions are still to be written.</p>","notes":[{"id":"why-caching-works","title":"Why caching works","html":"<p><strong>A cache is a small, fast copy of data that is slow to get from its real home.</strong> It works because access is skewed: a few items get most of the reads (the 80/20 rule, often even 95/5). Keep those few in RAM and most requests never touch the database.</p>\n<p>Numbers to remember: a Redis GET over a LAN takes about 0.2 to 1 ms. A Postgres indexed read is about 1 to 5 ms when warm and 10 ms or more under load. A cross-region call is 50 to 150 ms. The cache wins on latency and, more importantly, protects the database from load.</p>\n<figure class=\"flow\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 425.2 130\" width=\"425.2\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A cache hit never reaches the database\"><defs><marker id=\"fa\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\"/></marker></defs><path d=\"M102 65 L158 65\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><circle class=\"flow-dot\" r=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" opacity=\"0\"><animateMotion dur=\"2.4000000000000004s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" path=\"M102 65 L158 65\" keyPoints=\"0;0;1;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.000;0.333;1\" calcMode=\"linear\"/><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" dur=\"2.4000000000000004s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" values=\"0;1;1;0;0\" keyTimes=\"0;0.000;0.333;0.334;1\"/></circle><path d=\"M254 65 L310 34\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><circle class=\"flow-dot\" r=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" opacity=\"0\"><animateMotion dur=\"2.4000000000000004s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" path=\"M254 65 L310 34\" keyPoints=\"0;0;1;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.333;0.667;1\" calcMode=\"linear\"/><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" dur=\"2.4000000000000004s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" values=\"0;1;1;0;0\" keyTimes=\"0;0.333;0.667;0.668;1\"/></circle><path d=\"M254 65 L310 96\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><circle class=\"flow-dot\" r=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" opacity=\"0\"><animateMotion dur=\"2.4000000000000004s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" path=\"M254 65 L310 96\" keyPoints=\"0;0;1;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.333;0.667;1\" calcMode=\"linear\"/><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" dur=\"2.4000000000000004s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" values=\"0;1;1;0;0\" keyTimes=\"0;0.333;0.667;0.668;1\"/></circle><rect x=\"12\" y=\"43\" width=\"90\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"57\" y=\"69\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Client</text><rect x=\"158\" y=\"43\" width=\"96\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"206\" y=\"69\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">App server</text><rect x=\"310\" y=\"12\" width=\"103.2\" height=\"44\" rx=\"22\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"361.6\" y=\"38\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Redis cache</text><rect x=\"310\" y=\"74\" width=\"103.2\" height=\"44\" rx=\"22\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"361.6\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Postgres</text></svg><figcaption>A cache hit never reaches the database</figcaption></figure>\n<p><strong>Worked example.</strong> A product page gets 20,000 reads/s. Postgres can comfortably do about 5,000 simple reads/s. With a 95% hit ratio, only 1,000 reads/s reach Postgres. The cache turned an impossible load into an easy one without touching the database.</p>\n<p><strong>Industry examples.</strong> Facebook runs one of the largest Memcached fleets (their \"Scaling Memcache\" paper) so that MySQL sees a small fraction of reads. Twitter and Instagram cache timelines and counters in Redis/Memcached. Netflix EVCache holds hot metadata close to every region.</p>\n<p><strong>Everyday analogy.</strong> Keeping your most used spices on the kitchen counter instead of walking to the store each time.</p>\n<p><strong>Common interview traps.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Adding a cache with no idea of the hit ratio or what is hot. A cache for uniformly random access is nearly useless.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that a cache is a second source of truth that can disagree with the first.</li>\n<li>Caching to hide a missing index. Fix the query first.</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"cache-aside-lazy-loading","title":"Cache-aside (lazy loading)","html":"<p><strong>The application owns the logic: read the cache; on a miss read the database, then put the result in the cache.</strong> Writes go to the database and then delete (or update) the cache key.</p>\n<figure class=\"flow\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 558 130\" width=\"558\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Cache-aside read path on a miss\"><defs><marker id=\"fa\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\"/></marker></defs><path d=\"M400 65 L456 34\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><circle class=\"flow-dot\" r=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" opacity=\"0\"><animateMotion dur=\"3.2s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" path=\"M400 65 L456 34\" keyPoints=\"0;0;1;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.500;0.750;1\" calcMode=\"linear\"/><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" dur=\"3.2s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" values=\"0;1;1;0;0\" keyTimes=\"0;0.500;0.750;0.751;1\"/></circle><path d=\"M400 65 L456 96\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><circle class=\"flow-dot\" r=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" opacity=\"0\"><animateMotion dur=\"3.2s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" path=\"M400 65 L456 96\" keyPoints=\"0;0;1;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.500;0.750;1\" calcMode=\"linear\"/><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" dur=\"3.2s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" values=\"0;1;1;0;0\" keyTimes=\"0;0.500;0.750;0.751;1\"/></circle><rect x=\"304\" y=\"43\" width=\"96\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"352\" y=\"69\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">App server</text><rect x=\"456\" y=\"12\" width=\"90\" height=\"44\" rx=\"22\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"501\" y=\"38\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Cache</text><rect x=\"456\" y=\"74\" width=\"90\" height=\"44\" rx=\"22\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"501\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Database</text></svg><figcaption>Cache-aside read path on a miss</figcaption></figure>\n<pre class=\"shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark\" style=\"--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e\" tabindex=\"0\"><code><span class=\"line\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583\">def</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0\"> get_user</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\">(uid):</span></span>\n<span class=\"line\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\">    v </span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\"> redis.get(</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583\">f</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF\">\"user:</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF\">{</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\">uid</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF\">}</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF\">\"</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\">)</span></span>\n<span class=\"line\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583\">    if</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\"> v: </span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583\">return</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\"> json.loads(v)</span></span>\n<span class=\"line\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\">    row </span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\"> db.query(</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF\">\"select * from users where id=</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF\">%s</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF\">\"</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\">, uid)</span></span>\n<span class=\"line\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\">    redis.set(</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583\">f</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF\">\"user:</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF\">{</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\">uid</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF\">}</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF\">\"</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\">, json.dumps(row), </span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70\">ex</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF\">300</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\">)   </span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D\"># TTL 5 min</span></span>\n<span class=\"line\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583\">    return</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\"> row</span></span>\n<span class=\"line\"></span>\n<span class=\"line\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583\">def</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0\"> update_user</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\">(uid, data):</span></span>\n<span class=\"line\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\">    db.update(uid, data)</span></span>\n<span class=\"line\"><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\">    redis.delete(</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583\">f</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF\">\"user:</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF\">{</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\">uid</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF\">}</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF\">\"</span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8\">)      </span><span style=\"--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D\"># delete, do not set: see race below</span></span></code></pre>\n<p><strong>Why chosen.</strong> Simple, the cache holds only what is asked for, and a dead cache only slows things (the DB still answers). Alternative: read-through (cache library loads for you). It breaks with a cold cache (every first read is slow) and with a race: reader A reads old DB value, writer updates DB and deletes key, reader A then writes the old value into the cache. It stays stale until the TTL. Delete-on-write plus a short TTL limits the damage. Easy to change later: a two-way door.</p>\n<p><strong>Industry examples.</strong> Most Redis usage in the wild is cache-aside. Facebook's Memcache deletes keys on write and uses \"leases\" to fix the race above.</p>\n<p><strong>Everyday analogy.</strong> You check your notes first; if the answer is missing you look it up in the book and write it in your notes.</p>\n<p><strong>Common interview traps.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Updating the cache on write instead of deleting: two concurrent writers can leave the older value last.</li>\n<li>Not setting a TTL, so a missed invalidation lives forever.</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"read-through-write-through-write-behind","title":"Read-through, write-through, write-behind","html":"<p><strong>These move the caching logic from your code into the cache layer.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Read-through:</strong> the cache itself loads from the database on a miss. The app only talks to the cache.</li>\n<li><strong>Write-through:</strong> every write goes to the cache and the database synchronously, and returns when both succeed. Reads are always fresh, writes are slower (two hops).</li>\n<li><strong>Write-back (write-behind):</strong> write to the cache, return immediately, flush to the database later in batches. Very fast writes, but if the cache node dies before the flush, you lose data.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"flow\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 724.4 68\" width=\"724.4\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Write-back: the ack is fast, the DB catches up later\"><defs><marker id=\"fa\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\"/></marker></defs><path d=\"M102 34 L158 34\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><circle class=\"flow-dot\" r=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" opacity=\"0\"><animateMotion dur=\"4s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" path=\"M102 34 L158 34\" keyPoints=\"0;0;1;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.000;0.200;1\" calcMode=\"linear\"/><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" dur=\"4s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" values=\"0;1;1;0;0\" keyTimes=\"0;0.000;0.200;0.201;1\"/></circle><path d=\"M254 34 L310 34\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><circle class=\"flow-dot\" r=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" opacity=\"0\"><animateMotion dur=\"4s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" path=\"M254 34 L310 34\" keyPoints=\"0;0;1;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.200;0.400;1\" calcMode=\"linear\"/><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" dur=\"4s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" values=\"0;1;1;0;0\" keyTimes=\"0;0.200;0.400;0.401;1\"/></circle><path d=\"M400 34 L456 34\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><circle class=\"flow-dot\" r=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" opacity=\"0\"><animateMotion dur=\"4s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" path=\"M400 34 L456 34\" keyPoints=\"0;0;1;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.400;0.600;1\" calcMode=\"linear\"/><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" dur=\"4s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" values=\"0;1;1;0;0\" keyTimes=\"0;0.400;0.600;0.601;1\"/></circle><path d=\"M566.4 34 L622.4 34\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><circle class=\"flow-dot\" r=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" opacity=\"0\"><animateMotion dur=\"4s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" path=\"M566.4 34 L622.4 34\" keyPoints=\"0;0;1;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.600;0.800;1\" calcMode=\"linear\"/><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" dur=\"4s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" values=\"0;1;1;0;0\" keyTimes=\"0;0.600;0.800;0.801;1\"/></circle><rect x=\"12\" y=\"12\" width=\"90\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"57\" y=\"38\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Client</text><rect x=\"158\" y=\"12\" width=\"96\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"206\" y=\"38\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">App server</text><rect x=\"310\" y=\"12\" width=\"90\" height=\"44\" rx=\"22\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"355\" y=\"38\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Cache</text><rect x=\"456\" y=\"12\" width=\"110.4\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"511.2\" y=\"38\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Flush worker</text><rect x=\"622.4\" y=\"12\" width=\"90\" height=\"44\" rx=\"22\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"667.4\" y=\"38\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Database</text></svg><figcaption>Write-back: the ack is fast, the DB catches up later</figcaption></figure>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Write latency</th>\n<th>Data loss risk</th>\n<th>Typical use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cache-aside</td>\n<td>DB speed</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>General purpose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Write-through</td>\n<td>DB + cache</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>Read-heavy, freshness needed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Write-back</td>\n<td>Cache speed</td>\n<td>Yes, on crash</td>\n<td>Counters, likes, metrics</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Worked example.</strong> A \"like\" counter takes 50,000 increments/s on a viral post. Writing each to Postgres would melt one row. Write-back: Redis <code>INCR</code> and a worker flushes the total every 5 s, so Postgres gets 0.2 writes/s. Cost: up to 5 s of likes lost if Redis dies without persistence.</p>\n<p><strong>Industry examples.</strong> CPU caches use write-back. DynamoDB DAX is a write-through cache. Many view-count systems (YouTube-style) buffer counts and flush in batches.</p>\n<p><strong>Everyday analogy.</strong> Write-through: sign the register and the ledger together. Write-back: scribble on a sticky note and update the ledger tonight.</p>\n<p><strong>Common interview traps.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Choosing write-back for money or orders. Never for data you cannot lose.</li>\n<li>Ignoring ordering: batched flushes can reorder writes unless keyed carefully.</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"eviction-policies","title":"Eviction policies","html":"<p><strong>When the cache is full, it must throw something away. The policy decides what.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>LRU</strong> (least recently used): evict what was untouched longest. The default good choice.</li>\n<li><strong>LFU</strong> (least frequently used): evict the least popular. Better when a few items are hot for a long time; resists one-time scans.</li>\n<li><strong>FIFO / random:</strong> cheap, rarely best.</li>\n<li><strong>TTL-based:</strong> evict expired first.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Redis config: <code>maxmemory 8gb</code> and <code>maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru</code> (cache use), or <code>volatile-lru</code> (only keys with TTL), <code>allkeys-lfu</code>, <code>noeviction</code> (returns errors when full, for data stores). Redis LRU is approximated by sampling (<code>maxmemory-samples</code>, default 5), not an exact list.</p>\n<figure class=\"flow\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 439.6 130\" width=\"439.6\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Full cache evicts the coldest key to admit a new one\"><defs><marker id=\"fa\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\"/></marker></defs><path d=\"M102 65 L158 65\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><circle class=\"flow-dot\" r=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" opacity=\"0\"><animateMotion dur=\"2.4000000000000004s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" path=\"M102 65 L158 65\" keyPoints=\"0;0;1;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.000;0.333;1\" calcMode=\"linear\"/><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" dur=\"2.4000000000000004s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" values=\"0;1;1;0;0\" keyTimes=\"0;0.000;0.333;0.334;1\"/></circle><path d=\"M254 65 L310 34\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><circle class=\"flow-dot\" r=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" opacity=\"0\"><animateMotion dur=\"2.4000000000000004s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" path=\"M254 65 L310 34\" keyPoints=\"0;0;1;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.333;0.667;1\" calcMode=\"linear\"/><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" dur=\"2.4000000000000004s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" values=\"0;1;1;0;0\" keyTimes=\"0;0.333;0.667;0.668;1\"/></circle><path d=\"M254 65 L310 96\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><circle class=\"flow-dot\" r=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" opacity=\"0\"><animateMotion dur=\"2.4000000000000004s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" path=\"M254 65 L310 96\" keyPoints=\"0;0;1;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.333;0.667;1\" calcMode=\"linear\"/><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" dur=\"2.4000000000000004s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" values=\"0;1;1;0;0\" keyTimes=\"0;0.333;0.667;0.668;1\"/></circle><rect x=\"12\" y=\"43\" width=\"90\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"57\" y=\"69\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">New key</text><rect x=\"158\" y=\"43\" width=\"96\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"206\" y=\"69\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Cache full</text><rect x=\"310\" y=\"12\" width=\"117.60000000000001\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"368.8\" y=\"38\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Evict LRU key</text><rect x=\"310\" y=\"74\" width=\"117.60000000000001\" height=\"44\" rx=\"22\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"368.8\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Memory</text></svg><figcaption>Full cache evicts the coldest key to admit a new one</figcaption></figure>\n<p><strong>Worked example.</strong> 10M items of 1 KB = 10 GB of data but only 4 GB of RAM. If access is skewed so that the hottest 3M items take 90% of reads, LRU keeps them and the hit ratio is near 90%. A big one-time scan (a batch job reading everything) can flush the hot set from plain LRU; LFU or a separate cache for scans avoids this.</p>\n<p><strong>Industry examples.</strong> Memcached uses a segmented LRU per slab class. Redis added LFU in 4.0. Caffeine (Java) uses W-TinyLFU, a strong modern admission policy.</p>\n<p><strong>Everyday analogy.</strong> A small desk: the paper you have not touched for months goes back in the cabinet first.</p>\n<p><strong>Common interview traps.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Using <code>noeviction</code> for a cache and then seeing OOM write errors.</li>\n<li>Not leaving headroom: Redis fork for snapshots can double memory briefly; keep <code>maxmemory</code> around 60 to 75% of RAM.</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"ttl-and-invalidation","title":"TTL and invalidation","html":"<p><strong>\"There are only two hard things: cache invalidation and naming things.\"</strong> Invalidation is making sure the cache does not serve data that is wrong for too long.</p>\n<p>Options, from simplest to most precise:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>TTL only:</strong> accept staleness up to the TTL (30 s for a feed, 5 min for a profile, 24 h for static config). Add jitter (random +/-10%) so keys do not all expire together.</li>\n<li><strong>Delete on write:</strong> the writer removes the key after the DB commit.</li>\n<li><strong>Event-driven:</strong> DB changes (CDC, see the outbox notes) publish events, a consumer deletes keys. Works across services.</li>\n<li><strong>Versioned keys:</strong> <code>user:42:v17</code>. A change bumps the version; old keys just age out. No delete race.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"flow\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 752 68\" width=\"752\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Invalidation by event keeps many caches in sync\"><defs><marker id=\"fa\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\"/></marker></defs><path d=\"M136.8 34 L192.8 34\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><circle class=\"flow-dot\" r=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" opacity=\"0\"><animateMotion dur=\"4s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" path=\"M136.8 34 L192.8 34\" keyPoints=\"0;0;1;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.000;0.200;1\" calcMode=\"linear\"/><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" dur=\"4s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" values=\"0;1;1;0;0\" keyTimes=\"0;0.000;0.200;0.201;1\"/></circle><path d=\"M282.8 34 L338.8 34\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><circle class=\"flow-dot\" r=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" opacity=\"0\"><animateMotion dur=\"4s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" path=\"M282.8 34 L338.8 34\" keyPoints=\"0;0;1;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.200;0.400;1\" calcMode=\"linear\"/><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" dur=\"4s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" values=\"0;1;1;0;0\" keyTimes=\"0;0.200;0.400;0.401;1\"/></circle><path d=\"M434.8 34 L490.8 34\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><circle class=\"flow-dot\" r=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" opacity=\"0\"><animateMotion dur=\"4s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" path=\"M434.8 34 L490.8 34\" keyPoints=\"0;0;1;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.400;0.600;1\" calcMode=\"linear\"/><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" dur=\"4s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" values=\"0;1;1;0;0\" keyTimes=\"0;0.400;0.600;0.601;1\"/></circle><path d=\"M594 34 L650 34\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><circle class=\"flow-dot\" r=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" opacity=\"0\"><animateMotion dur=\"4s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" path=\"M594 34 L650 34\" keyPoints=\"0;0;1;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.600;0.800;1\" calcMode=\"linear\"/><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" dur=\"4s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" values=\"0;1;1;0;0\" keyTimes=\"0;0.600;0.800;0.801;1\"/></circle><rect x=\"12\" y=\"12\" width=\"124.8\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"74.4\" y=\"38\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Writer service</text><rect x=\"192.8\" y=\"12\" width=\"90\" height=\"44\" rx=\"22\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"237.8\" y=\"38\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Database</text><rect x=\"338.8\" y=\"12\" width=\"96\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"386.8\" y=\"38\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">CDC stream</text><rect x=\"490.8\" y=\"12\" width=\"103.2\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"542.4\" y=\"38\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Invalidator</text><rect x=\"650\" y=\"12\" width=\"90\" height=\"44\" rx=\"22\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"695\" y=\"38\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Cache</text></svg><figcaption>Invalidation by event keeps many caches in sync</figcaption></figure>\n<p><strong>Decision gravity.</strong> Choose staleness tolerance per data type, not globally. Prices and inventory need seconds; avatars can be hours. TTL-only is easy to reverse; a CDC-based invalidation pipeline is an investment that is hard to rip out.</p>\n<p><strong>Industry examples.</strong> Facebook tails the MySQL binlog to invalidate Memcache (the McSqueal daemon). CDNs use purge APIs plus TTL (Cloudflare, Fastly).</p>\n<p><strong>Everyday analogy.</strong> Milk in the fridge: a date stamp (TTL), or you throw it out the moment you buy a fresh carton (delete on write).</p>\n<p><strong>Common interview traps.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Delete before the DB commit: a reader can refill the old value in between.</li>\n<li>Same TTL on all keys, producing an expiry cliff (see avalanche in the next notes).</li>\n<li>Caching \"not found\" forever; use a short TTL for negatives.</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"hit-ratio-math","title":"Hit-ratio math","html":"<p><strong>Hit ratio = hits / (hits + misses).</strong> Average latency = hit_ratio x cache_latency + (1 - hit_ratio) x db_latency. And DB load = total QPS x (1 - hit_ratio).</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Cache 1 ms, DB 20 ms, 10,000 QPS.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Hit ratio</th>\n<th>Avg latency</th>\n<th>DB QPS</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>80%</td>\n<td>0.8 + 4.0 = 4.8 ms</td>\n<td>2,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>95%</td>\n<td>0.95 + 1.0 = 1.95 ms</td>\n<td>500</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>99%</td>\n<td>0.99 + 0.2 = 1.19 ms</td>\n<td>100</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Going from 95% to 99% cuts DB load 5x. The last few points matter hugely, so measure the <strong>miss</strong> rate, not the hit rate. Sizing: if the working set is 6 GB and you give 3 GB, expect the hit ratio to fall sharply. Redis metrics: <code>keyspace_hits</code>, <code>keyspace_misses</code>, <code>evicted_keys</code>; high evictions plus falling hit ratio means too little memory.</p>\n<figure class=\"flow\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 572.4 68\" width=\"572.4\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Fewer misses means far less database load\"><defs><marker id=\"fa\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\"/></marker></defs><path d=\"M102 34 L158 34\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><circle class=\"flow-dot\" r=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" opacity=\"0\"><animateMotion dur=\"3.2s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" path=\"M102 34 L158 34\" keyPoints=\"0;0;1;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.000;0.250;1\" calcMode=\"linear\"/><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" dur=\"3.2s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" values=\"0;1;1;0;0\" keyTimes=\"0;0.000;0.250;0.251;1\"/></circle><path d=\"M248 34 L304 34\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><circle class=\"flow-dot\" r=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" opacity=\"0\"><animateMotion dur=\"3.2s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" path=\"M248 34 L304 34\" keyPoints=\"0;0;1;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.250;0.500;1\" calcMode=\"linear\"/><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" dur=\"3.2s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" values=\"0;1;1;0;0\" keyTimes=\"0;0.250;0.500;0.501;1\"/></circle><path d=\"M414.4 34 L470.4 34\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><circle class=\"flow-dot\" r=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\" opacity=\"0\"><animateMotion dur=\"3.2s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" path=\"M414.4 34 L470.4 34\" keyPoints=\"0;0;1;1\" keyTimes=\"0;0.500;0.750;1\" calcMode=\"linear\"/><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" dur=\"3.2s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" values=\"0;1;1;0;0\" keyTimes=\"0;0.500;0.750;0.751;1\"/></circle><rect x=\"12\" y=\"12\" width=\"90\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"57\" y=\"38\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">10k QPS</text><rect x=\"158\" y=\"12\" width=\"90\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"203\" y=\"38\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Cache</text><rect x=\"304\" y=\"12\" width=\"110.4\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"359.2\" y=\"38\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">500 QPS miss</text><rect x=\"470.4\" y=\"12\" width=\"90\" height=\"44\" rx=\"22\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"515.4\" y=\"38\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" style=\"fill:var(--ink)\">Database</text></svg><figcaption>Fewer misses means far less database load</figcaption></figure>\n<p><strong>Industry examples.</strong> Facebook reports hit rates well above 90% and still focuses on the miss tail since it sets database capacity. CDN offload percent is the same metric.</p>\n<p><strong>Everyday analogy.</strong> A library lending desk: if 99 of 100 questions are answered from the front desk, the archive staff barely work.</p>\n<p><strong>Common interview traps.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Quoting the hit ratio without the key-space size or working-set size.</li>\n<li>Forgetting that a cold start (after a restart) has a hit ratio near 0 and can crush the DB; warm caches before shifting traffic.</li>\n</ul>"},{"id":"summary","title":"Summary","html":"<ul>\n<li>A cache protects the database and cuts latency because access is skewed; always estimate hit ratio and DB load.</li>\n<li>Cache-aside is the default: read cache, fall back to DB, delete on write, always set a TTL.</li>\n<li>Write-through is fresh but slower; write-back is fastest but can lose data.</li>\n<li>Use LRU/LFU with <code>maxmemory</code> headroom; avoid <code>noeviction</code> for caches.</li>\n<li>Invalidate by TTL with jitter, delete-on-write, events, or versioned keys; pick per data type.</li>\n<li>Hit-ratio math: DB QPS = QPS x miss rate; 95% to 99% is a 5x drop in DB load.</li>\n</ul>"}],"questions":[]}